{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc | sed -n '1,340p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream.rpc\n     2\t  \"Transport-neutral, polling RPC state machines for DaoStream v2.\n     3\t\n     4\t   This layer deliberately owns no socket, scheduler, atom, promise, callback,\n     5\t   retry policy, or transport registry.  A composition supplies two explicit\n     6\t   handles (a request writer and a response reader), owns the returned state,\n     7\t   and chooses when to step `request!` and `poll!`.\n     8\t\n     9\t   The wire values are `dao.stream.apply` envelopes: a request is\n    10\t   `{:dao.stream.apply/id :dao.stream.apply/op :dao.stream.apply/args}` and an\n    11\t   answer is `{:dao.stream.apply/id :dao.stream.apply/ok <value>}` or\n    12\t   `{:dao.stream.apply/id :dao.stream.apply/error {:dao.stream.apply/code\n    13\t   :dao.stream.apply/message}}`.  Envelopes stay open and are retained and\n    14\t   forwarded whole.  Events, diagnostics, and completions are this client's\n    15\t   own state and keep the `:dao.stream.rpc/*` keys; only the request and\n    16\t   answer values they embed are apply envelopes.\n    17\t\n    18\t   Id allocation is client policy layered over apply's opaque correlation id:\n    19\t   ids are self-minted random safe integers, not a sequential counter, so\n    20\t   callers sharing one answers stream must not collide on a caller-chosen id\n    21\t   (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md, section 5).  A decoded answer whose id\n    22\t   is not `safe-id?` is a diagnostic here, not an apply-level rejection.  The\n    23\t   number of outstanding requests is bounded by `:max-outstanding`.\"\n    24\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    25\t            [dao.stream.apply :as apply2]))\n    26\t\n    27\t\n    28\t;; =============================================================================\n    29\t;; Public data vocabulary\n    30\t;; =============================================================================\n    31\t\n    32\t(def max-safe-id\n    33\t  \"Largest integer that remains exactly representable by every supported host.\"\n    34\t  9007199254740991)\n    35\t\n    36\t\n    37\t(def event-key :dao.stream.rpc/event)\n    38\t(def response-key :dao.stream.rpc/response)\n    39\t(def diagnostic-key :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic)\n    40\t(def code-key :dao.stream.rpc/code)\n    41\t\n    42\t\n    43\t(def max-id-retries\n    44\t  \"Bound on re-rolling a self-minted id after a collision, before allocation\n    45\t   fails through the existing terminal path.\"\n    46\t  8)\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\t(def default-max-outstanding\n    50\t  \"Outstanding-request limit used when `client-state` is not given one.\"\n    51\t  64)\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\t(defn response-event\n    55\t  [response]\n    56\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/response response-key response})\n    57\t\n    58\t\n    59\t(defn diagnostic-event\n    60\t  ([code]\n    61\t   (diagnostic-event code nil))\n    62\t  ([code value]\n    63\t   {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic\n    64\t    diagnostic-key {code-key code\n    65\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/value value}}))\n    66\t\n    67\t\n    68\t(defn ignore-event\n    69\t  []\n    70\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/ignore})\n    71\t\n    72\t\n    73\t(defn safe-id?\n    74\t  \"True for an integer which round-trips without precision loss across CLJ,\n    75\t   CLJS, and CLJD.  IDs are routing tokens, not application values.\"\n    76\t  [id]\n    77\t  (and (integer? id) (<= 0 id max-safe-id)))\n    78\t\n    79\t\n    80\t;; =============================================================================\n    81\t;; Request/answer values: thin aliases of the dao.stream.apply envelope\n    82\t;; =============================================================================\n    83\t\n    84\t(def request-value apply2/request)\n    85\t(def request-value? apply2/request?)\n    86\t(def request-id apply2/request-id)\n    87\t(def request-op apply2/request-op)\n    88\t(def request-args apply2/request-args)\n    89\t(def success-answer apply2/success-response)\n    90\t(def error-answer apply2/error-response)\n    91\t(def answer-id apply2/response-id)\n    92\t(def answer-ok apply2/response-ok)\n    93\t(def answer-error apply2/response-error)\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\t(defn answer?\n    97\t  \"True for an apply response this client can correlate: `apply/response?`\n    98\t   (which validates an error body) plus this client's `safe-id?` policy.\"\n    99\t  [v]\n   100\t  (and (apply2/response? v) (safe-id? (apply2/response-id v))))\n   101\t\n   102\t\n   103\t(defn answer-ok?\n   104\t  [answer]\n   105\t  (contains? answer apply2/ok-key))\n   106\t\n   107\t\n   108\t;; =============================================================================\n   109\t;; Explicit client state\n   110\t;; =============================================================================\n   111\t\n   112\t(defn- random-safe-id\n   113\t  []\n   114\t  #?(:clj (long (* max-safe-id (rand)))\n   115\t     :cljs (Math/floor (* max-safe-id (rand)))\n   116\t     :cljd (.floor ^double (* max-safe-id (rand)))))\n   117\t\n   118\t\n   119\t(defn client-state\n   120\t  \"Create caller-owned RPC client state.\n   121\t\n   122\t   `writer` is the request path. `reader` and `response-cursor` are the\n   123\t   independent response path.  No cursor is fabricated here; a caller\n   124\t   over a dao.stream.remote reflection passes `stream/anchor-newest`\n   125\t   (the request/response service's own mint, docs/design/\n   126\t   dao.stream.remote.md section 5) and `poll!` resolves it, retrying\n   127\t   while the reflection answers a retryable mint -- the same pattern\n   128\t   `dao.jing.content.step/mint-cursor` uses for the content client.\n   129\t\n   130\t   `opts` may carry `:max-outstanding`, a positive integer bounding accepted\n   131\t   requests awaiting an answer (default `default-max-outstanding`).  At the\n   132\t   limit `request!` answers `:dao.stream.rpc/backpressure` without allocating\n   133\t   an id or appending.\"\n   134\t  ([writer reader response-cursor]\n   135\t   (client-state writer reader response-cursor {}))\n   136\t  ([writer reader response-cursor opts]\n   137\t   (let [limit (get opts :max-outstanding default-max-outstanding)]\n   138\t     (when-not (and (integer? limit) (pos? limit))\n   139\t       (throw (ex-info \"max-outstanding must be a positive integer\"\n   140\t                       {:dao.stream.rpc/max-outstanding limit})))\n   141\t     {:writer writer\n   142\t      :reader reader\n   143\t      :cursor response-cursor\n   144\t      :max-outstanding limit\n   145\t      :outstanding {}\n   146\t      :unsent nil\n   147\t      :completed []\n   148\t      :diagnostics []\n   149\t      :terminal nil})))\n   150\t\n   151\t\n   152\t(def init-client client-state)\n   153\t\n   154\t\n   155\t(defn- rpc-result\n   156\t  [outcome state & {:as extra}]\n   157\t  (merge {:dao.stream.rpc/outcome outcome\n   158\t          :dao.stream.rpc/state state}\n   159\t         extra))\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t(defn- valid-operation-result\n   163\t  [op result]\n   164\t  (if (stream/valid-outcome? op result)\n   165\t    result\n   166\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   167\t     :dao.stream/error :dao.stream/invalid-operation-result}))\n   168\t\n   169\t\n   170\t(defn- completion\n   171\t  [request & {:keys [response reason]}]\n   172\t  (cond-> {:dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   173\t           :dao.stream.rpc/op (:op request)\n   174\t           :dao.stream.rpc/args (:args request)}\n   175\t    response (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/response response)\n   176\t    reason (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason)))\n   177\t\n   178\t\n   179\t(defn- append-completion\n   180\t  [state request & {:as fields}]\n   181\t  (update state :completed conj (apply completion request (mapcat identity fields))))\n   182\t\n   183\t\n   184\t(defn- append-diagnostic\n   185\t  [state code value]\n   186\t  (update state :diagnostics conj {code-key code\n   187\t                                   :dao.stream.rpc/value value}))\n   188\t\n   189\t\n   190\t(defn- ids-in-use?\n   191\t  [state id]\n   192\t  (or (= id (get-in state [:unsent :id]))\n   193\t      (contains? (:outstanding state) id)\n   194\t      (boolean (some #(= id (:dao.stream.rpc/id %)) (:completed state)))))\n   195\t\n   196\t\n   197\t;; Defined below with the other completion bookkeeping; allocation failure is\n   198\t;; a terminal transition and owes the same conservative loss as every other.\n   199\t(declare lose-outstanding)\n   200\t\n   201\t\n   202\t(defn- allocation-failure\n   203\t  [state code]\n   204\t  (let [state (-> state\n   205\t                  (lose-outstanding :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error true)\n   206\t                  (append-diagnostic code nil))]\n   207\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error state\n   208\t                :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state)))))\n   209\t\n   210\t\n   211\t(defn- allocate-request\n   212\t  \"Self-mint a random safe id in [0, max-safe-id], re-rolling on a collision\n   213\t   with an id already in use by this state, up to `max-id-retries` times.\n   214\t   This is the request/answer service's own id space (docs/design/\n   215\t   dao.stream.remote.md section 5): callers sharing one answers stream must\n   216\t   not collide on a caller-chosen id, so the id is not a sequential counter.\"\n   217\t  [state op args]\n   218\t  (loop [attempt 0]\n   219\t    (if (>= attempt max-id-retries)\n   220\t      (allocation-failure state :dao.stream.rpc/id-collision)\n   221\t      (let [id (random-safe-id)]\n   222\t        (if (ids-in-use? state id)\n   223\t          (recur (inc attempt))\n   224\t          (let [request {:id id\n   225\t                         :op op\n   226\t                         :args args\n   227\t                         :encoded (request-value id op args)}]\n   228\t            (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/allocated\n   229\t                        (assoc state :unsent request))))))))\n   230\t\n   231\t\n   232\t(defn- accept-unsent\n   233\t  [state request]\n   234\t  (-> state\n   235\t      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   236\t      (assoc-in [:outstanding (:id request)]\n   237\t                {:op (:op request) :args (:args request)})))\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\t(defn- attempt-unsent\n   241\t  [state]\n   242\t  (let [request (:unsent state)\n   243\t        append-result (valid-operation-result\n   244\t                        :append!\n   245\t                        (stream/append! (:writer state) (:encoded request)))\n   246\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome append-result)]\n   247\t    (case outcome\n   248\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   249\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/requested\n   250\t                  (accept-unsent state request)\n   251\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   252\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)\n   253\t\n   254\t      :dao.stream/full\n   255\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/pending-request state\n   256\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   257\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)\n   258\t\n   259\t      (:dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/invalid-value :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   260\t      (let [state (-> state\n   261\t                      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   262\t                      (append-completion request :reason outcome))]\n   263\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable state\n   264\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   265\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason outcome\n   266\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result))\n   267\t\n   268\t      ;; A malformed writer answer becomes transport-error in\n   269\t      ;; valid-operation-result, so this branch is defensive only.\n   270\t      (let [state (-> state\n   271\t                      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   272\t                      (append-completion request :reason :dao.stream/transport-error))]\n   273\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable state\n   274\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   275\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/transport-error\n   276\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)))))\n   277\t\n   278\t\n   279\t(defn request!\n   280\t  \"Attempt one request append, returning an explicit next state.\n   281\t\n   282\t   If a previous append was full, this retries the exact already-allocated\n   283\t   envelope and ignores `op`/`args`; it never allocates a replacement id.  The\n   284\t   function makes one append attempt at most and never spins on `:full`.\n   285\t\n   286\t   With `:max-outstanding` requests already outstanding, a new request answers\n   287\t   `:dao.stream.rpc/backpressure`: no id is allocated and nothing is appended.\"\n   288\t  [state op args]\n   289\t  (cond\n   290\t    (:terminal state)\n   291\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n   292\t\n   293\t    (:unsent state)\n   294\t    (attempt-unsent state)\n   295\t\n   296\t    (not (and (keyword? op) (vector? args)))\n   297\t    (let [state (append-diagnostic state :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request\n   298\t                                   {:op op :args args})]\n   299\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request state\n   300\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   301\t\n   302\t    (>= (count (:outstanding state)) (:max-outstanding state))\n   303\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/backpressure state)\n   304\t\n   305\t    :else\n   306\t    (let [allocated (allocate-request state op args)]\n   307\t      (if (= :dao.stream.rpc/allocated (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome allocated))\n   308\t        (attempt-unsent (:dao.stream.rpc/state allocated))\n   309\t        allocated))))\n   310\t\n   311\t\n   312\t(defn unsent?\n   313\t  \"True while an allocated request is still owed one accepted append.  A caller\n   314\t   that allocates ids from this state must not submit new work while it holds:\n   315\t   `request!` retries the unsent envelope and ignores the operation it is given.\"\n   316\t  [state]\n   317\t  (some? (:unsent state)))\n   318\t\n   319\t\n   320\t(defn abandon-unsent\n   321\t  \"Give up on an allocated-but-unsent request, appending its completion with\n   322\t   `reason`.\n   323\t\n   324\t   A composition that changes what the writer *is* — an operator disconnect, a\n   325\t   rebind onto a fresh attachment — owns the decision that the retained\n   326\t   envelope no longer belongs to the new binding.  Abandoning it here reports\n   327\t   the loss on the ordinary completion path, so it is neither silently dropped\n   328\t   nor resent in place of the caller's next request.\"\n   329\t  ([state] (abandon-unsent state :dao.stream.rpc/abandoned))\n   330\t  ([state reason]\n   331\t   (if-let [request (:unsent state)]\n   332\t     (-> state\n   333\t         (assoc :unsent nil)\n   334\t         (append-completion request :reason reason))\n   335\t     state)))\n   336\t\n   337\t\n   338\t;; =============================================================================\n   339\t;; Response decoding and correlation\n   340\t;; =============================================================================\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'defn.*(poll|submit-input)|request\"'!|rebind'\"' src/cljc/yin/repl/{adapter,connect}.cljc; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc | sed -n '1,240p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc | sed -n '1,240p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'defn.*(poll|submit-input)|request\"'!|rebind'\"' src/cljc/yin/repl/{adapter,connect}.cljc; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc | sed -n '1,240p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc | sed -n '1,240p'\"","aggregated_output":"src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:123:(defn submit-input\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:129:   most one append attempt through `rpc/request!`; it never waits or retries.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:158:    (let [rpc-result (rpc/request! (:yin.repl.adapter/rpc state)\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:203:(defn poll-responses\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:18:   both reflection attaches, then `rpc/rebind`s the retained RPC client onto\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:486:   adapter, both reflections attached again, then `rpc/rebind`.\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:516:             client-key (rpc/rebind client\n     1\t(ns yin.repl.adapter\n     2\t  \"A small, host-neutral bridge between a line-oriented Yin REPL and the\n     3\t   DaoStream RPC state machine.\n     4\t\n     5\t   This is deliberately not a replacement for `yin.repl`: it does not create a\n     6\t   socket, evaluate a form, print, own an atom, or invoke a callback.  A driver\n     7\t   owns the state returned here.  Local shell commands are emitted as data for\n     8\t   that driver to evaluate locally; every other input string becomes one\n     9\t   `:op/eval` request through the explicit RPC client state.\"\n    10\t  (:require #?(:cljd [clojure.edn :as edn]\n    11\t               :clj [clojure.edn :as edn]\n    12\t               :cljs [cljs.reader :as reader])\n    13\t            [dao.stream.rpc :as rpc]))\n    14\t\n    15\t\n    16\t(def eval-operation :op/eval)\n    17\t\n    18\t\n    19\t(def local-command-heads\n    20\t  \"Commands which retain the legacy REPL's local meaning when a remote RPC\n    21\t   client is present.  The adapter emits their original input; it does not\n    22\t   interpret or execute them.\"\n    23\t  #{'connect 'disconnect 'help 'quit 'repl-state})\n    24\t\n    25\t\n    26\t(def rejected-command-heads\n    27\t  \"Commands the REPL does not have.  `(telemetry)` is out in every form, so\n    28\t   the adapter names the rejection as an event rather than doing nothing locally\n    29\t   or sending the line to a remote evaluator that would report it as a missing\n    30\t   var.\"\n    31\t  #{'telemetry})\n    32\t\n    33\t\n    34\t(defn state\n    35\t  \"Create adapter state around explicit, caller-owned RPC client state.\n    36\t   `:events` is an unpublished outbox; use `take-events` to consume it once.\"\n    37\t  [rpc-state]\n    38\t  {:yin.repl.adapter/rpc rpc-state\n    39\t   :yin.repl.adapter/events []})\n    40\t\n    41\t\n    42\t(def init-state state)\n    43\t\n    44\t\n    45\t(defn eval-request\n    46\t  \"Construct the request value used for a non-local input.  Normal callers\n    47\t   use `submit-input`, which delegates id allocation and append handling to\n    48\t   RPC.\"\n    49\t  [id input]\n    50\t  (rpc/request-value id eval-operation [input]))\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t(defn- read-forms\n    54\t  \"Read the line with a non-evaluating reader on every host.  Sniffing an input\n    55\t   line for a shell command must never run it; anything the data reader rejects\n    56\t   is ordinary source for the remote evaluator.\"\n    57\t  [input]\n    58\t  #?(:cljd (edn/read-string (str \"[\" input \"]\"))\n    59\t     :clj (edn/read-string (str \"[\" input \"]\"))\n    60\t     :cljs (reader/read-string (str \"[\" input \"]\"))))\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\t(defn command-form\n    64\t  \"Exactly one well-formed shell-command form, otherwise nil.  Reader failures\n    65\t   intentionally mean remote input: ordinary language source must reach the\n    66\t   remote evaluator unchanged.\"\n    67\t  [input]\n    68\t  (when (string? input)\n    69\t    (try\n    70\t      (let [forms (read-forms input)\n    71\t            form (when (= 1 (count forms)) (first forms))]\n    72\t        (when (and (seq? form) (symbol? (first form))) form))\n    73\t      (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ nil))))\n    74\t\n    75\t\n    76\t(defn- command-head\n    77\t  \"The head symbol of one well-formed shell-command form, otherwise nil.\"\n    78\t  [input]\n    79\t  (first (command-form input)))\n    80\t\n    81\t\n    82\t(defn local-command\n    83\t  \"Return the local command symbol for one well-formed local shell command,\n    84\t   otherwise nil.\"\n    85\t  [input]\n    86\t  (let [head (command-head input)]\n    87\t    (when (contains? local-command-heads head) head)))\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t(defn rejected-command\n    91\t  \"Return the command symbol for one well-formed command this slice rejects,\n    92\t   otherwise nil.\"\n    93\t  [input]\n    94\t  (let [head (command-head input)]\n    95\t    (when (contains? rejected-command-heads head) head)))\n    96\t\n    97\t\n    98\t(defn- adapter-result\n    99\t  [outcome state & {:as extra}]\n   100\t  (merge {:yin.repl.adapter/outcome outcome\n   101\t          :yin.repl.adapter/state state}\n   102\t         extra))\n   103\t\n   104\t\n   105\t(defn- append-event\n   106\t  [state event]\n   107\t  (update state :yin.repl.adapter/events conj event))\n   108\t\n   109\t\n   110\t(defn- request-outcome\n   111\t  [rpc-outcome]\n   112\t  (case rpc-outcome\n   113\t    :dao.stream.rpc/requested :yin.repl.adapter/requested\n   114\t    :dao.stream.rpc/pending-request :yin.repl.adapter/pending-request\n   115\t    :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable :yin.repl.adapter/request-undeliverable\n   116\t    :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request :yin.repl.adapter/invalid-input\n   117\t    :dao.stream.rpc/backpressure :yin.repl.adapter/backpressure\n   118\t    :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error :yin.repl.adapter/terminal\n   119\t    :dao.stream.rpc/terminal :yin.repl.adapter/terminal\n   120\t    :yin.repl.adapter/transport-result))\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\t(defn submit-input\n   124\t  \"Advance one input transition.\n   125\t\n   126\t   A local command becomes an explicit event and leaves RPC state untouched, as\n   127\t   does a command this slice rejects.  Other strings are submitted as a\n   128\t   `:op/eval` request.  This function makes at\n   129\t   most one append attempt through `rpc/request!`; it never waits or retries.\"\n   130\t  [state input]\n   131\t  (cond\n   132\t    (not (string? input))\n   133\t    (let [event {:yin.repl.adapter/event :yin.repl.adapter/invalid-input\n   134\t                 :yin.repl.adapter/input input}\n   135\t          state (append-event state event)]\n   136\t      (adapter-result :yin.repl.adapter/invalid-input state\n   137\t                      :yin.repl.adapter/event event))\n   138\t\n   139\t    (rejected-command input)\n   140\t    (let [command (rejected-command input)\n   141\t          event {:yin.repl.adapter/event :yin.repl.adapter/rejected-command\n   142\t                 :yin.repl.adapter/command command\n   143\t                 :yin.repl.adapter/input input}\n   144\t          state (append-event state event)]\n   145\t      (adapter-result :yin.repl.adapter/rejected-command state\n   146\t                      :yin.repl.adapter/event event))\n   147\t\n   148\t    (local-command input)\n   149\t    (let [command (local-command input)\n   150\t          event {:yin.repl.adapter/event :yin.repl.adapter/local-command\n   151\t                 :yin.repl.adapter/command command\n   152\t                 :yin.repl.adapter/input input}\n   153\t          state (append-event state event)]\n   154\t      (adapter-result :yin.repl.adapter/local-command state\n   155\t                      :yin.repl.adapter/event event))\n   156\t\n   157\t    :else\n   158\t    (let [rpc-result (rpc/request! (:yin.repl.adapter/rpc state)\n   159\t                                   eval-operation\n   160\t                                   [input])\n   161\t          state (assoc state :yin.repl.adapter/rpc\n   162\t                       (:dao.stream.rpc/state rpc-result))]\n   163\t      (adapter-result (request-outcome (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome rpc-result))\n   164\t                      state\n   165\t                      :yin.repl.adapter/rpc-result rpc-result))))\n   166\t\n   167\t\n   168\t(defn- completion-event\n   169\t  [completion]\n   170\t  (let [base {:yin.repl.adapter/id (:dao.stream.rpc/id completion)\n   171\t              :yin.repl.adapter/op (:dao.stream.rpc/op completion)\n   172\t              :yin.repl.adapter/args (:dao.stream.rpc/args completion)}]\n   173\t    (if-let [response (:dao.stream.rpc/response completion)]\n   174\t      (if (rpc/answer-ok? response)\n   175\t        (assoc base\n   176\t               :yin.repl.adapter/event :yin.repl.adapter/response\n   177\t               :yin.repl.adapter/value (rpc/answer-ok response))\n   178\t        (assoc base\n   179\t               :yin.repl.adapter/event :yin.repl.adapter/response\n   180\t               :yin.repl.adapter/error (rpc/answer-error response)))\n   181\t      (assoc base\n   182\t             :yin.repl.adapter/event :yin.repl.adapter/lost\n   183\t             :yin.repl.adapter/reason (:dao.stream.rpc/reason completion)))))\n   184\t\n   185\t\n   186\t(defn publish-completions\n   187\t  \"Move RPC's completion outbox into the REPL event outbox exactly once.\n   188\t   The returned RPC state has an empty completion vector, so a later driver tick\n   189\t   cannot publish the same correlated response or loss again.\"\n   190\t  [state]\n   191\t  (let [[completions rpc-state] (rpc/take-completed (:yin.repl.adapter/rpc state))\n   192\t        events (mapv completion-event completions)\n   193\t        state (-> state\n   194\t                  (assoc :yin.repl.adapter/rpc rpc-state)\n   195\t                  (update :yin.repl.adapter/events into events))]\n   196\t    (adapter-result (if (seq events)\n   197\t                      :yin.repl.adapter/published\n   198\t                      :yin.repl.adapter/idle)\n   199\t                    state\n   200\t                    :yin.repl.adapter/events events)))\n   201\t\n   202\t\n   203\t(defn poll-responses\n   204\t  \"Poll up to `budget` response values, then publish any correlated completion\n   205\t   values as REPL events.  `:blocked` returns immediately through `rpc/poll!`.\n   206\t   No host asynchronous value, callback, or scheduler is introduced.\"\n   207\t  ([state] (poll-responses state 1))\n   208\t  ([state budget]\n   209\t   (let [rpc-result (rpc/poll! (:yin.repl.adapter/rpc state) budget)\n   210\t         state (assoc state :yin.repl.adapter/rpc\n   211\t                      (:dao.stream.rpc/state rpc-result))\n   212\t         published (publish-completions state)\n   213\t         state (:yin.repl.adapter/state published)\n   214\t         rpc-outcome (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome rpc-result)\n   215\t         outcome (cond\n   216\t                   (seq (:yin.repl.adapter/events published))\n   217\t                   :yin.repl.adapter/responded\n   218\t\n   219\t                   (= rpc-outcome :dao.stream.rpc/idle)\n   220\t                   :yin.repl.adapter/idle\n   221\t\n   222\t                   (= rpc-outcome :dao.stream.rpc/terminal)\n   223\t                   :yin.repl.adapter/terminal\n   224\t\n   225\t                   :else :yin.repl.adapter/transport-result)]\n   226\t     (adapter-result outcome state\n   227\t                     :yin.repl.adapter/rpc-result rpc-result\n   228\t                     :yin.repl.adapter/events\n   229\t                     (:yin.repl.adapter/events published)))))\n   230\t\n   231\t\n   232\t(defn take-events\n   233\t  \"Return `[events next-state]`, clearing the adapter event outbox exactly once.\n   234\t   Drivers interpret local-command events locally and render response/loss events\n   235\t   in their own host loop.\"\n   236\t  [state]\n   237\t  [(:yin.repl.adapter/events state)\n   238\t   (assoc state :yin.repl.adapter/events [])])\n     1\t(ns yin.repl.connect\n     2\t  \"The client side of the DaoStream Yin REPL, over the request-and-response\n     3\t   service of docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md section 5.\n     4\t\n     5\t   One connection is one explicit value.  `open` canonicalizes the URL to the\n     6\t   request-target form `dao.stream.ws.md` defines, dials one WebSocket\n     7\t   channel through `dao.stream.ws-project`, and attaches two\n     8\t   `dao.stream.remote` reflections over it -- one of `\\\"yin.repl/requests\\\"`\n     9\t   (this end writes), one of `\\\"yin.repl/answers\\\"` (this end reads) -- both\n    10\t   sharing the one link the dial establishes.  `attach!` over\n    11\t   `dao.stream.remote` answers ok at once: that is the contract's deferred\n    12\t   remote confirmation, not a promise that the server has resolved anything,\n    13\t   so a connection is reported `:established` immediately rather than\n    14\t   waiting on an asynchronous transport event.\n    15\t\n    16\t   The dial is reusable across reconnects: `close!` closes the underlying\n    17\t   channel handle, and reattaching redials the same `attach!` and repeats\n    18\t   both reflection attaches, then `rpc/rebind`s the retained RPC client onto\n    19\t   the fresh pair -- which keeps its response cursor and its outstanding\n    20\t   bookkeeping, exactly as before.  Terminal status\n    21\t   (`:detached`/`:ended`/`:not-found`/`:transport-error`) is observed from\n    22\t   the RPC client's own `:terminal`, translated by `dao.stream.rpc`'s\n    23\t   reflection-read translation; `observe-terminal` turns that into the one\n    24\t   connection notice each terminal reason publishes exactly once.\"\n    25\t  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n    26\t            [dao.data :as data]\n    27\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\n    28\t            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ring]\n    29\t            [dao.stream.rpc :as rpc]\n    30\t            [dao.stream.ws :as ws]\n    31\t            [dao.stream.ws-project :as ws-project]\n    32\t            [yin.repl.host.common :as host-common]))\n    33\t\n    34\t\n    35\t;; =============================================================================\n    36\t;; Composition constants\n    37\t;; =============================================================================\n    38\t\n    39\t(def traffic-capacity\n    40\t  \"Declared capacity of the boundary's one traffic medium, in elements.  It is\n    41\t   reused across reconnects because a client boundary has at most one active\n    42\t   attachment at a time.\"\n    43\t  8192)\n    44\t\n    45\t\n    46\t(def traffic-admission\n    47\t  {:retention :evict-oldest\n    48\t   :capacity traffic-capacity\n    49\t   :value-domain :portable-values})\n    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\t(def url-prefix \"daostream:\")\n    53\t(def ws-scheme \"ws://\")\n    54\t(def secure-scheme \"wss://\")\n    55\t(def default-port 8080)\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(def repl-path\n    59\t  \"The path an empty URL path means for this REPL (D3).  An explicit `/`\n    60\t   remains `/`; only an absent path becomes the served REPL stream.\"\n    61\t  \"/repl\")\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    64\t(def service-identity\n    65\t  \"The logical identity of the WebSocket channel a REPL endpoint serves.  A\n    66\t   descriptor names a served stream, so a client that only typed a URL still\n    67\t   needs one; a stable service name keeps `attach!` honest across endpoint\n    68\t   restarts.\"\n    69\t  \"yin.repl/repl\")\n    70\t\n    71\t\n    72\t(def requests-identity\n    73\t  \"The mirror table identity the server enters with surface #{:writer}: a\n    74\t   client's reflection of it writes eval requests.\"\n    75\t  \"yin.repl/requests\")\n    76\t\n    77\t\n    78\t(def answers-identity\n    79\t  \"The mirror table identity the server enters with surface #{:reader}: a\n    80\t   client's reflection of it reads answers.\"\n    81\t  \"yin.repl/answers\")\n    82\t\n    83\t\n    84\t(def ^:private diagnostic-bounds\n    85\t  \"Bound on operator-facing diagnostic text built from unbounded internal\n    86\t   state (raw connect URLs).\"\n    87\t  {:depth 3 :items 8 :chars 200})\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t(def outcome-key :yin.repl.connect/outcome)\n    91\t(def connection-key :yin.repl.connect/connection)\n    92\t(def client-key :yin.repl.connect/client)\n    93\t(def descriptor-key :yin.repl.connect/descriptor)\n    94\t(def message-key :yin.repl.connect/message)\n    95\t(def event-key :yin.repl.connect/event)\n    96\t(def text-key :yin.repl.connect/text)\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\t;; =============================================================================\n   100\t;; Canonical request-target paths\n   101\t;; =============================================================================\n   102\t\n   103\t(def ^:private ascii-printable\n   104\t  \" !\\\"#$%&'()*+,-./0123456789:;<=>?@ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ[\\\\]^_`abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz{|}~\")\n   105\t\n   106\t\n   107\t(def ^:private unreserved\n   108\t  (set (map str (seq \"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789-._~\"))))\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   111\t(def ^:private hex-values\n   112\t  (merge (zipmap (map str (seq \"0123456789\")) (range 10))\n   113\t         (zipmap (map str (seq \"abcdef\")) (range 10 16))\n   114\t         (zipmap (map str (seq \"ABCDEF\")) (range 10 16))))\n   115\t\n   116\t\n   117\t(def ^:private digits (set (map str (seq \"0123456789\"))))\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\t(defn- at\n   121\t  \"One character of `s` as a one-character string, so nothing here depends on a\n   122\t   host's character representation.\"\n   123\t  [s i]\n   124\t  (subs s i (inc i)))\n   125\t\n   126\t\n   127\t(defn- octet->char\n   128\t  [code]\n   129\t  (when (and (<= 32 code) (<= code 126))\n   130\t    (subs ascii-printable (- code 32) (- code 31))))\n   131\t\n   132\t\n   133\t(defn- percent-canonical\n   134\t  \"Decode percent-encoded unreserved octets and upper-case every remaining\n   135\t   percent hex pair.  An encoded slash is not unreserved, so it stays encoded.\"\n   136\t  [s]\n   137\t  (let [n (count s)]\n   138\t    (loop [i 0\n   139\t           out []]\n   140\t      (if (>= i n)\n   141\t        (str/join out)\n   142\t        (let [c (at s i)\n   143\t              hi (when (< (+ i 2) n) (get hex-values (at s (+ i 1))))\n   144\t              lo (when (< (+ i 2) n) (get hex-values (at s (+ i 2))))]\n   145\t          (if (and (= \"%\" c) hi lo)\n   146\t            (let [decoded (octet->char (+ (* 16 hi) lo))]\n   147\t              (if (contains? unreserved decoded)\n   148\t                (recur (+ i 3) (conj out decoded))\n   149\t                (recur (+ i 3) (conj out (str \"%\" (str/upper-case (subs s (inc i) (+ i 3))))))))\n   150\t            (recur (inc i) (conj out c))))))))\n   151\t\n   152\t\n   153\t(defn- drop-last-segment\n   154\t  [out]\n   155\t  (if-let [i (str/last-index-of out \"/\")]\n   156\t    (subs out 0 i)\n   157\t    \"\"))\n   158\t\n   159\t\n   160\t(defn- next-segment-end\n   161\t  [s]\n   162\t  (or (str/index-of s \"/\" 1) (count s)))\n   163\t\n   164\t\n   165\t(defn- remove-dot-segments\n   166\t  \"RFC 3986's remove_dot_segments, so repeated slashes and a trailing slash stay\n   167\t   significant while `.` and `..` do not reach the exact served-path lookup.\"\n   168\t  [path]\n   169\t  (loop [in path\n   170\t         out \"\"]\n   171\t    (cond\n   172\t      (= \"\" in) out\n   173\t      (str/starts-with? in \"../\") (recur (subs in 3) out)\n   174\t      (str/starts-with? in \"./\") (recur (subs in 2) out)\n   175\t      (str/starts-with? in \"/./\") (recur (str \"/\" (subs in 3)) out)\n   176\t      (= \"/.\" in) (recur \"/\" out)\n   177\t      (str/starts-with? in \"/../\") (recur (str \"/\" (subs in 4)) (drop-last-segment out))\n   178\t      (= \"/..\" in) (recur \"/\" (drop-last-segment out))\n   179\t      (= \".\" in) (recur \"\" out)\n   180\t      (= \"..\" in) (recur \"\" out)\n   181\t      :else (let [end (next-segment-end in)]\n   182\t              (recur (subs in end) (str out (subs in 0 end)))))))\n   183\t\n   184\t\n   185\t(defn- path-component\n   186\t  \"The URI path component of `raw`: everything before the first query or\n   187\t   fragment delimiter.\"\n   188\t  [raw]\n   189\t  (let [raw (str raw)\n   190\t        cut (->> [(str/index-of raw \"?\") (str/index-of raw \"#\")]\n   191\t                 (filter some?)\n   192\t                 (reduce min (count raw)))]\n   193\t    (subs raw 0 cut)))\n   194\t\n   195\t\n   196\t(defn canonical-path\n   197\t  \"The canonical, non-empty absolute request-target path of `raw`.\n   198\t\n   199\t   Query and fragment are ignored for lookup and absent from the descriptor,\n   200\t   dot segments are removed, percent-encoded unreserved octets are decoded, and\n   201\t   remaining percent hex digits are upper-case.  An empty URI path becomes `/`;\n   202\t   substituting the REPL's own default for an absent path is `repl-target`'s\n   203\t   job, and happens before this generic canonicalizer runs.\"\n   204\t  [raw]\n   205\t  (let [path (path-component raw)\n   206\t        path (if (str/starts-with? path \"/\") path (str \"/\" path))\n   207\t        path (percent-canonical (remove-dot-segments path))]\n   208\t    (if (= \"\" path) \"/\" path)))\n   209\t\n   210\t\n   211\t(defn repl-target\n   212\t  \"The REPL wrapper's rule: an empty raw URL path names `/repl`, and an explicit\n   213\t   `/` remains `/`.  Emptiness is a fact about the URI path component, so\n   214\t   `ws://host:port?x=1` carries no path and names `/repl` too.\"\n   215\t  [raw]\n   216\t  (if (= \"\" (path-component raw))\n   217\t    repl-path\n   218\t    (canonical-path raw)))\n   219\t\n   220\t\n   221\t;; =============================================================================\n   222\t;; URL to descriptor\n   223\t;; =============================================================================\n   224\t\n   225\t(defn- failure\n   226\t  [outcome message]\n   227\t  {outcome-key outcome message-key message})\n   228\t\n   229\t\n   230\t(defn- parse-port\n   231\t  [text]\n   232\t  (when (and (seq text) (every? #(contains? digits %) (map str (seq text))))\n   233\t    (loop [i 0 acc 0]\n   234\t      (if (>= i (count text))\n   235\t        (when (pos? acc) acc)\n   236\t        (recur (inc i) (+ (* 10 acc) (get hex-values (at text i))))))))\n   237\t\n   238\t\n   239\t(defn- authority-end\n   240\t  [s]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
