{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc | sed -n '275,390p;440,610p'\"","aggregated_output":"   275\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/transport-error\n   276\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)))))\n   277\t\n   278\t\n   279\t(defn request!\n   280\t  \"Attempt one request append, returning an explicit next state.\n   281\t\n   282\t   If a previous append was full, this retries the exact already-allocated\n   283\t   envelope and ignores `op`/`args`; it never allocates a replacement id.  The\n   284\t   function makes one append attempt at most and never spins on `:full`.\n   285\t\n   286\t   With `:max-outstanding` requests already outstanding, a new request answers\n   287\t   `:dao.stream.rpc/backpressure`: no id is allocated and nothing is appended.\"\n   288\t  [state op args]\n   289\t  (cond\n   290\t    (:terminal state)\n   291\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n   292\t\n   293\t    (:unsent state)\n   294\t    (attempt-unsent state)\n   295\t\n   296\t    (not (and (keyword? op) (vector? args)))\n   297\t    (let [state (append-diagnostic state :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request\n   298\t                                   {:op op :args args})]\n   299\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request state\n   300\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   301\t\n   302\t    (>= (count (:outstanding state)) (:max-outstanding state))\n   303\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/backpressure state)\n   304\t\n   305\t    :else\n   306\t    (let [allocated (allocate-request state op args)]\n   307\t      (if (= :dao.stream.rpc/allocated (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome allocated))\n   308\t        (attempt-unsent (:dao.stream.rpc/state allocated))\n   309\t        allocated))))\n   310\t\n   311\t\n   312\t(defn unsent?\n   313\t  \"True while an allocated request is still owed one accepted append.  A caller\n   314\t   that allocates ids from this state must not submit new work while it holds:\n   315\t   `request!` retries the unsent envelope and ignores the operation it is given.\"\n   316\t  [state]\n   317\t  (some? (:unsent state)))\n   318\t\n   319\t\n   320\t(defn abandon-unsent\n   321\t  \"Give up on an allocated-but-unsent request, appending its completion with\n   322\t   `reason`.\n   323\t\n   324\t   A composition that changes what the writer *is* — an operator disconnect, a\n   325\t   rebind onto a fresh attachment — owns the decision that the retained\n   326\t   envelope no longer belongs to the new binding.  Abandoning it here reports\n   327\t   the loss on the ordinary completion path, so it is neither silently dropped\n   328\t   nor resent in place of the caller's next request.\"\n   329\t  ([state] (abandon-unsent state :dao.stream.rpc/abandoned))\n   330\t  ([state reason]\n   331\t   (if-let [request (:unsent state)]\n   332\t     (-> state\n   333\t         (assoc :unsent nil)\n   334\t         (append-completion request :reason reason))\n   335\t     state)))\n   336\t\n   337\t\n   338\t;; =============================================================================\n   339\t;; Response decoding and correlation\n   340\t;; =============================================================================\n   341\t\n   342\t(defn- answer-diagnostic\n   343\t  \"Diagnostic for a value that is not an answer this client accepts: an apply\n   344\t   response with an id outside `safe-id?` is `unsafe-response-id`; anything\n   345\t   else, including an invalid error body, is `malformed-response`.\"\n   346\t  [value]\n   347\t  (diagnostic-event (if (apply2/response? value)\n   348\t                      :dao.stream.rpc/unsafe-response-id\n   349\t                      :dao.stream.rpc/malformed-response)\n   350\t                    value))\n   351\t\n   352\t\n   353\t(defn- normalize-event\n   354\t  [value]\n   355\t  (cond\n   356\t    (nil? value) (ignore-event)\n   357\t    (answer? value) (response-event value)\n   358\t    (and (map? value) (keyword? (get value event-key))) value\n   359\t    :else (answer-diagnostic value)))\n   360\t\n   361\t\n   362\t(defn- decode-value\n   363\t  [_state value]\n   364\t  (try\n   365\t    (normalize-event value)\n   366\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   367\t      (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/decode-error value))))\n   368\t\n   369\t\n   370\t(defn- lose-outstanding\n   371\t  [state reason terminal?]\n   372\t  (let [state (reduce (fn [s [id request]]\n   373\t                        (append-completion s (assoc request :id id) :reason reason))\n   374\t                      state\n   375\t                      (:outstanding state))]\n   376\t    (cond-> (assoc state :outstanding {})\n   377\t      terminal? (assoc :terminal reason))))\n   378\t\n   379\t\n   380\t(defn handle-event\n   381\t  \"Apply one decoded RPC event to explicit client state.\n   382\t\n   383\t   `response-event`, `diagnostic-event`, and `ignore-event` are the events a\n   384\t   composition constructs; a bare answer value is also accepted.  This\n   385\t   function has no cursor operation and is useful for testing adapters\n   386\t   independently from a response medium.\"\n   387\t  [state event]\n   388\t  (let [event (normalize-event event)\n   389\t        kind (get event event-key)]\n   390\t    (cond\n   440\t    :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone :dao.stream.apply/detached\n   441\t    :dao.stream.remote/no-surface :dao.stream.apply/no-surface\n   442\t    :dao.stream.remote/oversize :dao.stream.apply/oversize\n   443\t    :dao.stream.apply/transport-error))\n   444\t\n   445\t\n   446\t(defn- terminal-lost\n   447\t  \"Translate a non-ok, non-blocked, non-gap read or mint result into the\n   448\t   client's terminal vocabulary and complete every outstanding request\n   449\t   with it.  Shared by `poll-read`'s `next` failures and `mint-cursor`'s\n   450\t   terminal mint failures, so a reasoned not-found or channel-gone\n   451\t   reaches the terminal the same way regardless of which operation\n   452\t   the reflection answered it to.\"\n   453\t  [state result]\n   454\t  (let [reason (case (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n   455\t                 :dao.stream/end :dao.stream.apply/ended\n   456\t                 :dao.stream/transport-error (transport-error-reason result)\n   457\t                 :dao.stream.apply/transport-error)\n   458\t        state (lose-outstanding state reason true)]\n   459\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   460\t                :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason\n   461\t                :dao.stream.rpc/read result)))\n   462\t\n   463\t\n   464\t(defn- mint-cursor\n   465\t  \"Resolve an anchor-keyword cursor through the reader.  A retryable\n   466\t   answer -- `:dao.stream/transport-error` carrying `:dao.stream/retry?\n   467\t   true` -- leaves the anchor in place for a later poll (dao.jing.content.\n   468\t   step/mint-cursor is the precedent this mirrors).  Any other non-ok\n   469\t   outcome is a reasoned terminal failure: a reflection can answer\n   470\t   `not-found` or `channel-gone` while minting a cursor\n   471\t   (dao.stream.remote.cljc's `refl-cursor`), and that must not be\n   472\t   mistaken for still-pending.  Returns `[status cursor result]`, status\n   473\t   one of `:ok`, `:retry`, `:terminal`; `result` is retained for\n   474\t   diagnostics either way.\"\n   475\t  [reader anchor]\n   476\t  (let [r (stream/cursor reader anchor)\n   477\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome r)]\n   478\t    (cond\n   479\t      (= :dao.stream/ok outcome) [:ok (:dao.stream/cursor r) r]\n   480\t\n   481\t      (and (= :dao.stream/transport-error outcome) (:dao.stream/retry? r))\n   482\t      [:retry anchor r]\n   483\t\n   484\t      :else [:terminal anchor r])))\n   485\t\n   486\t\n   487\t(defn- poll-read\n   488\t  [state]\n   489\t  (let [read-result (valid-operation-result :next (stream/next (:reader state) (:cursor state)))\n   490\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome read-result)]\n   491\t    (case outcome\n   492\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   493\t      ;; Advance before decoding. A malformed or unsolicited element is thereby\n   494\t      ;; consumed exactly once and cannot poison polling.\n   495\t      (handle-event (assoc state :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   496\t                    (decode-value state (:dao.stream/value read-result)))\n   497\t\n   498\t      :dao.stream/blocked\n   499\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result)\n   500\t\n   501\t      :dao.stream/gap\n   502\t      (let [state (-> state\n   503\t                      (assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   504\t                      (lose-outstanding :dao.stream/gap false))]\n   505\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   506\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/gap\n   507\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result))\n   508\t\n   509\t      (terminal-lost state read-result))))\n   510\t\n   511\t\n   512\t(defn poll!\n   513\t  \"Read at most `budget` response-medium elements.\n   514\t\n   515\t   A `:dao.stream/blocked` result yields immediately; this function never loops\n   516\t   waiting for a response.  Each successful read installs the exact successor\n   517\t   cursor returned by that handle before decoding the element.\"\n   518\t  ([state] (poll! state 1))\n   519\t  ([state budget]\n   520\t   (cond\n   521\t     (:terminal state) (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n   522\t     (not (and (integer? budget) (pos? budget)))\n   523\t     (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state)\n   524\t     :else\n   525\t     (loop [remaining budget\n   526\t            state state\n   527\t            last-result nil]\n   528\t       (cond\n   529\t         (zero? remaining) (or last-result (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state))\n   530\t\n   531\t         ;; The response cursor is an unresolved anchor: settling it is not\n   532\t         ;; a read and does not spend the budget, exactly as\n   533\t         ;; dao.jing.content.step/poll's own anchor branch does not.\n   534\t         (contains? stream/standard-anchors (:cursor state))\n   535\t         (let [[status cursor r] (mint-cursor (:reader state) (:cursor state))]\n   536\t           (case status\n   537\t             :ok (recur remaining (assoc state :cursor cursor) last-result)\n   538\t             :retry (or last-result\n   539\t                        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state :dao.stream.rpc/read r))\n   540\t             :terminal (terminal-lost state r)))\n   541\t\n   542\t         :else\n   543\t         (let [result (poll-read state)\n   544\t               next-state (:dao.stream.rpc/state result)\n   545\t               outcome (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome result)]\n   546\t           (if (or (:terminal next-state)\n   547\t                   (= outcome :dao.stream.rpc/idle))\n   548\t             result\n   549\t             (recur (dec remaining) next-state result))))))))\n   550\t\n   551\t\n   552\t(defn take-completed\n   553\t  \"Return `[completions next-state]`, clearing the unpublished completion\n   554\t   outbox exactly once.  The caller owns publication and must call this only\n   555\t   from its single state-owning driver step.\"\n   556\t  [state]\n   557\t  [(:completed state) (assoc state :completed [])])\n   558\t\n   559\t\n   560\t(defn take-diagnostics\n   561\t  \"Return `[diagnostics next-state]`, clearing the unpublished diagnostic outbox\n   562\t   exactly once.  Diagnostics are bounded by publication exactly as completions\n   563\t   are: a noisy shared response medium can produce one per read, so a caller\n   564\t   that never publishes would grow this vector for the session's lifetime.\"\n   565\t  [state]\n   566\t  [(:diagnostics state) (assoc state :diagnostics [])])\n   567\t\n   568\t\n   569\t(defn rebind\n   570\t  \"Replace a detached client's writer (and optionally reader) without creating\n   571\t   a cursor or replacing the random id allocator. Only `/detached` is\n   572\t   reconnectable; other terminal reasons remain terminal.\"\n   573\t  ([state writer]\n   574\t   (if (= :dao.stream.apply/detached (:terminal state))\n   575\t     (assoc state :writer writer :terminal nil)\n   576\t     state))\n   577\t  ([state writer reader]\n   578\t   (if (= :dao.stream.apply/detached (:terminal state))\n   579\t     (assoc state :writer writer :reader reader :terminal nil)\n   580\t     state)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
